WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.189 --> 00:00:16.240
I started at YC in 2013 as YC's
first Director of Outreach.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.340
And that meant outreach to
potential applicants, so

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.420
people who were starting companies,
so basically people like you.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:28.350
And when I started at YC, we'd grown
totally organically up until that point.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.100
So that meant that most of the people
applying to YC had heard about YC

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.510
through Paul Graham's essays, through
reading Jessica Livingston's book or

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.855
because they were Hacker News readers.

00:00:38.855 --> 00:00:45.460
So, Hacker News started out as
a Reddit for tech and startup news.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.890
But if you've ever read the comments,

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:53.270
it might not shock you to realize that
most of the Hacker News community is male.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.420
And if I had to guess, even though we
don't have those specific demographics,

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.160
I would guess white male.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.521
So, basically, I realized, and
I think Paul and Jessica realized also,

00:01:02.521 --> 00:01:06.192
that there are smart,
hardworking people all over the world,

00:01:06.192 --> 00:01:10.590
who should be starting startups,
who weren't users of Hacker News.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.020
So I was tasked with figuring
out where are these smart and

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.990
hardworking people, and how do we
get them to consider applying to YC?

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:24.005
How do we get them to consider starting
a company, if they hadn't started already?

00:01:24.005 --> 00:01:29.419
And so, one of the things I end up working
a lot on at YC, is finding ways to recruit

00:01:29.419 --> 00:01:34.660
founders from what is typically
underrepresented communities in tech.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.610
And then I also think a lot about what do
we do to support those communities once

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.430
they're here, once they're starting
companies, once they're in Silicon Valley?

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:48.108
So, to be honest, I was not supper
aware of the particular challenges that

00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:53.623
a lot of women and people of color
face in tech when I started in 2013.

00:01:53.623 --> 00:01:58.412
So in 2012, I know, the big mantra,
the big thing everyone

00:01:58.412 --> 00:02:02.951
said is tech is a meritocracy,
and I wanted to believe that.

00:02:02.951 --> 00:02:04.287
I wanted to believe.

00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:09.576
And I have been personally very,
very lucky to work

00:02:09.576 --> 00:02:14.920
in an environment that is
very supportive of me.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.200
So working at YC for example with Paul and
Jessica at first, then with Sam and

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.028
the other partners, I was really
lucky that they took a chance on me.

00:02:23.028 --> 00:02:28.500
That they gave me a lot of autonomy and
a lot of opportunity to do great work.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.862
But, even in a place where I think I
have had a pretty great experience,

00:02:32.862 --> 00:02:37.810
I've had moments and I've had experiences
that make me wonder whether my life,

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:41.693
whether my job, would've been
easier if I were a man, right?

00:02:41.693 --> 00:02:46.421
I've struggled with a lot of anxiety
about, am I meant to be here,

00:02:46.421 --> 00:02:48.454
am I qualified for this job?

00:02:48.454 --> 00:02:50.806
And there are certain experiences, for

00:02:50.806 --> 00:02:55.663
example, not so long ago, there was a
founder, for example, who had a meltdown.

00:02:55.663 --> 00:02:59.554
He had a meltdown about something that
frankly I or none of the partners had

00:02:59.554 --> 00:03:03.530
any control over, and frankly he didn't
have any control over it either.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.600
But what does he do,
he calls me and he yells.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.960
He starts yelling, uncontrollably.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.150
And as I'm on the phone with this founder,
I'm thinking why am I the target for this?

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.000
I don't see him yelling at other partners.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.846
I don't see him yelling at other male
partners and it just really bothered me,

00:03:18.846 --> 00:03:21.013
and it really set me back for
a little while.

00:03:21.013 --> 00:03:26.472
And so, frankly in the work that
I do with the founders, that

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:33.810
is what sort of really makes me realize
that we have such a long way to go.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:38.040
So for example, just in the last batch,
I had founders tell me stories about

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.040
being asked out on dates by customers or
by investors.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.945
We had one woman who was told by an
investor that she needed to find a white

00:03:46.945 --> 00:03:49.455
male co-founder to pitch for her.

00:03:49.455 --> 00:03:54.056
We had one woman at demo day, she was a
non-profit founder who an investor came up

00:03:54.056 --> 00:03:57.590
and said hey I'll give you
$2000 if you babysit my kids.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.336
And I'm like, what the ****?

00:04:00.336 --> 00:04:05.060
[LAUGH] I think what strikes me
is that we are living in 2017.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.920
We are living in one of
the most liberal places,

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.890
most progressive places,
in the United States, if not the world.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:16.300
So what I believe is that we have
an opportunity here to do better.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.350
And I'm excited for you, because I think
that one of the things that's most

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.750
exciting is that you have this opportunity
to build a culture from scratch.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.829
Companies are basically like
their own countries, and

00:04:29.829 --> 00:04:33.390
you've the opportunity to build
the world that you want to live in.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.125
So some of the best founders
I worked with are ambitious,

00:04:37.125 --> 00:04:41.550
they're idealistic,
they sign up to solve these big problems.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:45.670
Because they see that they're unsatisfied
with something they see in the world and

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:49.480
they think like, hey, I have
the opportunity here to make real change.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:55.190
So you end up creating an ecosystem with
its own rules, its own structure, and

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:56.560
its own culture.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.660
And that will be modeled based on your
personality and your beliefs as founders.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.910
So when Sam asked me
to host this workshop,

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:07.620
I realized that YC
doesn't have a playbook.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.977
We don't have a playbook that we just
go to that walks early-stage companies

00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:14.264
through how to think about D&amp;I issues.

00:05:14.264 --> 00:05:18.221
But if you've paid any attention to the
tech news in the past few years, you've

00:05:18.221 --> 00:05:22.580
realized that there's a momentum building
up around the conversations around D&amp;I.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:26.260
And basically, I think you all
realize at this point, as I have,

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:31.260
that most companies really feel and
wish that they had

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:35.920
thought about this stuff earlier on
in the life cycles of their company.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:41.120
So what I decided to do was I decided to
talk to as many YC companies as possible.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.680
I wanted to find out how they thought
about D&amp;I at their companies,

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:46.930
why it was important to them.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.920
I want to figure out what was working,
what was exciting them about the space?

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.190
And I think most importantly perhaps,
what is not working?

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.416
And so I brought six of the people here
today that I thought I had the most

00:05:58.416 --> 00:06:00.785
interesting and
thoughtful conversations with.

00:06:00.785 --> 00:06:04.370
And they're going to share their
stories and observations with you.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:06.638
So the folks up here, I believe,

00:06:06.638 --> 00:06:11.104
will have basically enough knowledge
in the space to fill a book.

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:14.410
But for today we have pretty simple goals.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.860
So first, we want to better define
what diversity and inclusion are.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.109
So I think the words are sort of so
overused these days,

00:06:21.109 --> 00:06:24.652
especially in the media,
that they become a little empty.

00:06:24.652 --> 00:06:28.570
And so we'll talk briefly about how
we define each of these terms in this

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:30.110
context.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.610
Next, we'll talk about
why D&amp;I was important for

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.630
these companies to think about and
when they started thinking about it.

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:38.330
And a little bit about why you
should think about it right now,

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:40.800
even at the very earliest stages.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.260
And then finally,

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:47.020
we want to talk about how to build a
culture that embraces hard conversations.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.605
So diversity and inclusion even for
me as a woman and

00:06:49.605 --> 00:06:52.982
as a person of color can feel like
really scary and awkward topics.

00:06:52.982 --> 00:06:58.057
And I have a lot of anxiety about
offending or angering other people,

00:06:58.057 --> 00:07:02.840
that's what scares and
kind of like stresses me out the most.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:07.410
But I also realize that having these hard
conversations with your co-founders and

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:09.020
with the folks that work with you,

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.330
these are some of the most
important conversations to have.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.105
So many of you today, and many of you
watching, are pre-hiring, so we're

00:07:17.105 --> 00:07:21.512
not going to get into the nitty-gritty of
things like writing job descriptions or

00:07:21.512 --> 00:07:23.108
how to conduct interviews.

00:07:23.108 --> 00:07:25.481
But I will share a page of resources for

00:07:25.481 --> 00:07:29.570
you that you can reference when
you're are ready for that stage.

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:34.447
But today I think we'll talk about things
that you can bake into your culture from

00:07:34.447 --> 00:07:38.534
day one, that will help you lay
the foundation of building a company

00:07:38.534 --> 00:07:41.720
where everyone is set up
to do their best work.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:46.022
So hopefully you'll leave here today
with at least one thing that you

00:07:46.022 --> 00:07:50.810
feel excited and like prepared
to try to implement or try out.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:56.050
So really quickly, diversity and
inclusion, these are broad concepts and

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:00.830
they're going to mean different things to
different companies and different people.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:05.702
So one of my favorite definitions
that I've heard is by Verna Myers,

00:08:05.702 --> 00:08:08.990
who is a lawyer who does a lot of
D&amp;I consulting for the legal world.

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:13.493
She describes diversity as
being invited to the party,

00:08:13.493 --> 00:08:17.241
whereas, inclusion is
being asked to dance.

00:08:17.241 --> 00:08:19.210
And so diversity in this context,

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:23.660
I think, will relate to finding and
hiring people from a range of backgrounds.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:28.910
What diversity is not,
is a code word for gender or race.

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:31.860
And so diversity, I think,
can mean a lot of things.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:35.450
It can mean different
academic backgrounds.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:39.500
It can mean different ages, national
origins, religions, work experiences,

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:40.970
perspectives.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:45.820
And it's going to be up to you to decide
what diversity means for your company,

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:47.710
and what you'd like to focus on first.

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:50.320
And what you decide to focus on first,
is going to change.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:52.940
It's going to change,
depending on certain factors.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.550
It might depend on where
your company is based, and

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:57.988
what the demographics
are like in that region.

00:08:57.988 --> 00:09:01.431
It might depend on your product,
what is going to be important.

00:09:01.431 --> 00:09:04.318
And so
if you want to build the diverse team,

00:09:04.318 --> 00:09:07.540
you have to think about it deliberately.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:13.030
Because one trap that happens is that
people focus on hiring individuals.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:15.930
So what happens is you come up with
a template in your mind for what

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:20.870
an ideal employee would look like and then
suddenly you have an homogeneous team.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:24.910
So many of the alumni I've talked
to who are doing this really well

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:27.090
have said they have had
to stretch themselves.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:30.481
They've had to think about hiring a team.

00:09:30.481 --> 00:09:37.380
So basically a great team won't all
have the same strengths and weaknesses.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.260
The individuals won't have
the same strengths and weaknesses.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.330
They will have skills that
complement each other.

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:46.380
And if you think about it in some of your
contexts, if you're trying to choose

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:51.460
a co-founder for example, you wouldn't
want to work with a clone of yourself.

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:54.845
You would ideally want to work with
someone who had complimentary skills.

00:09:54.845 --> 00:09:59.786
And so here let's see, moving on to

00:09:59.786 --> 00:10:04.576
inclusion, let's see, sorry.

00:10:04.576 --> 00:10:09.392
Most founders I talked to say that
after fundraising sort of the ongoing

00:10:09.392 --> 00:10:13.899
hardest part of their jobs is hiring,
it's hiring great talent.

00:10:13.899 --> 00:10:18.264
But one thing that a people related
challenge that is kind of less talked

00:10:18.264 --> 00:10:19.584
about is retaining.

00:10:19.584 --> 00:10:22.620
How do you get great talent
to stay at your company?

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:26.020
So you can to the work to recruit talent,
but if your culture is garbage,

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:28.500
they're probably not going to stay for
very long.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.817
So the Kapor Center just
released a tech leavers survey,

00:10:31.817 --> 00:10:35.770
which talks about why people
voluntarily leave their jobs in tech.

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:39.430
And I recommend you read the whole thing,
its really interesting and

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:40.954
it's not incredibly long.

00:10:40.954 --> 00:10:44.593
These are are just four main takeaways.

00:10:44.593 --> 00:10:47.322
As the Kapor center has
laid out in their study,

00:10:47.322 --> 00:10:51.086
this is a huge problem and
it is a very expensive problem for tech.

00:10:51.086 --> 00:10:54.862
And they have seen that D&amp;I initiatives,
if done right,

00:10:54.862 --> 00:10:58.112
can improve culture and
really reduce turnover.

00:10:58.112 --> 00:11:01.811
And so Jen from Leaver,
who you'll meet soon,

00:11:01.811 --> 00:11:06.120
was talking about what does
the flip side look like?

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.656
There's a lot of these terrible cases of
what happens when you don't think about

00:11:09.656 --> 00:11:10.384
culture early.

00:11:10.384 --> 00:11:15.166
But if you do build a culture,
that is great what do you get?

00:11:15.166 --> 00:11:17.883
You get a place where people have strong,

00:11:17.883 --> 00:11:21.790
personal bonds they
are loyal to your mission.

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:23.974
They stay a long time.

00:11:23.974 --> 00:11:26.374
And so Lever I've been specifically
really impressed with.

00:11:26.374 --> 00:11:30.377
They are at about 100 employees and
they have a 50-50% gender split.

00:11:30.377 --> 00:11:34.626
And I hope when Jen gets up here today she
talks a little bit more about the positive

00:11:34.626 --> 00:11:38.502
benefits that she has seen at Lever as
a result of them having thought about

00:11:38.502 --> 00:11:41.399
culture and inclusion from a very,
very early stage.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:47.890
So in all my conversations with YC alumni,
the thing I heard repeated over and over

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:54.200
again is that inclusion is just one aspect
of building a great culture from scratch.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:58.340
Inclusion is really about building
a culture where everyone can feel safe.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:01.936
And where people don't have to shy away
from having these really tough and

00:12:01.936 --> 00:12:03.976
awkward, sometimes, conversations.

00:12:03.976 --> 00:12:08.875
So we'll talk about some of those ways
that companies are tackling that challenge

00:12:08.875 --> 00:12:13.774
on the panel, but first let's talk really
briefly about why you might even want to

00:12:13.774 --> 00:12:16.046
start thinking about this right now.

00:12:16.046 --> 00:12:19.450
So there's a great McKinsey
study that came out in

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:24.246
2015 that I included in the resources
doc that I suggest you read.

00:12:24.246 --> 00:12:26.960
But here are some thoughts from YC alumni.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:30.227
So Porter Braswell is the founder
of a company called Jopwell and

00:12:30.227 --> 00:12:34.174
they connect Black and Latin-accent and
Native American people with jobs.

00:12:34.174 --> 00:12:37.504
And he described why it's
important in this way.

00:12:37.504 --> 00:12:41.354
How can you build world changing
products or services if you don't have

00:12:41.354 --> 00:12:45.797
a complete understanding of the different
perspective that exists in the world?

00:12:45.797 --> 00:12:50.351
The founders of ExVivo though another
YC company learned this the hard way

00:12:50.351 --> 00:12:51.860
they said.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:55.210
We figured out early that if we hired
people who came from the same academic

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.600
background, they approached
problems in the same way.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:02.920
ExVivo brought up a really great
example to illustrate that point.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:06.140
And I want to kind of do
a quick experiment here.

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:11.510
So how many of you keep your ketchup
in the fridge, in the refrigerator?

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:17.670
How many of you keep your
ketchup in the cupboard?

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:18.308
Whoa!

00:13:18.308 --> 00:13:22.490
[LAUGH] So there's a great episode of
the podcast Reply All where they interview

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:27.420
Scott Page, who's a professor at the
University of Michigan who studies teams.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:30.880
So Scott says that language,
age, geography,

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:35.240
personal hardship, that is all going
to influence how we solve problems.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:37.430
And he gave this example of
where you keep your ketchup.

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:42.260
So it turns out after he did
this study if you are British or

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:44.430
African American from the South,

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:48.230
there's a strong probability that you
will keep your ketchup in the cupboard.

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:49.920
And if you are not either of those things,

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.980
there's a strong probability that you
will keep your ketchup in the fridge.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:55.930
So you might think why does this matter?

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.810
This seems like a really trivial point.

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:00.340
But what happens if you
run out of ketchup?

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:03.252
So if you are a ketchup in the fridge
person, you open the fridge and

00:14:03.252 --> 00:14:06.684
you might replace it with the things that
are next to the ketchup in the fridge,

00:14:06.684 --> 00:14:08.263
which are usually mustard or mayo.

00:14:08.263 --> 00:14:12.386
But if you are a ketchup in the cupboard
person you might open the cupboard and

00:14:12.386 --> 00:14:17.220
realize, I'm out of ketchup, so
you might put malt vinegar on your fries.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:22.490
And so I love that example because,
basically, it showcases that the more

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:25.940
diverse backgrounds you have represented,
the more associations you get,

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:29.918
the more ways that you have more
pathways to solving problems.

00:14:29.918 --> 00:14:32.570
So, honestly, before hearing the study,

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:35.780
I had no ideas that humans even
put their ketchup in the cupboard.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:37.365
I didn't even know people
like that existed.

00:14:37.365 --> 00:14:40.610
So I was like wow,
this is blowing my mind.

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:41.367
So anyway,

00:14:41.367 --> 00:14:46.154
recruiting might not be something
that you're thinking about right now.

00:14:46.154 --> 00:14:53.211
But inclusion is in the words of Makinde
who you'll meet in a couple minutes.

00:14:53.211 --> 00:14:56.369
Inclusion is something
you can tank early on.

00:14:56.369 --> 00:15:00.989
So there are really well known stories
about what happens when you don't start

00:15:00.989 --> 00:15:03.311
thinking about this at an early stage.

00:15:03.311 --> 00:15:07.110
There is issues with the ride sharing
company who will not be named, but

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:08.835
let's also talk about Airbnb.

00:15:08.835 --> 00:15:13.076
So last year at the Fortune Brainstorm
Tech Conference, the founder of

00:15:13.076 --> 00:15:17.812
Airbnb Brian Chesky was talking about how
they were struggling with claims that

00:15:17.812 --> 00:15:22.993
Airbnb enabled discrimination especially
against the African American community.

00:15:22.993 --> 00:15:26.769
And he admitted,
I think we were late to this issue.

00:15:26.769 --> 00:15:30.946
He said we realize that while we were
building a platform we were three white

00:15:30.946 --> 00:15:32.549
guys, Joe, Nate, Brian.

00:15:32.549 --> 00:15:35.555
And he said that there were a lot
of things we didn't think about.

00:15:35.555 --> 00:15:40.240
So there were a lot of steps that
they're having to re-evaluate now.

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:45.023
So now that we've talked briefly
about DNI, how to define it,

00:15:45.023 --> 00:15:47.831
why you might want to think about it.

00:15:47.831 --> 00:15:52.074
I would love to invite the panel up to
share their stories and observations, and

00:15:52.074 --> 00:15:55.888
have them introduce themselves as well,
and let's bring you guys up.

00:16:02.064 --> 00:16:04.304
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Thank you Stephen.

00:16:09.452 --> 00:16:14.803
&gt;&gt; Okay, No, I'm not-
&gt;&gt; So we'll be sharing be the mics,

00:16:14.803 --> 00:16:18.044
because [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; So yeah, you can just pass them.

00:16:18.044 --> 00:16:20.544
Is this awkward if I'm standing,
is that okay?

00:16:20.544 --> 00:16:23.383
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, so

00:16:23.383 --> 00:16:27.419
I would love you to go down the line and
introduce your name,

00:16:27.419 --> 00:16:31.647
your company, your role, and
how big your company is today.

00:16:31.647 --> 00:16:35.370
&gt;&gt; Sure my name is Ashu,
I'm one of the co-founders of Make School.

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:37.660
We're building a college for
computer science.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:43.330
The company is about 25 people and I'm,
me and my co-founder are co-CEO's.

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:47.320
&gt;&gt; Hi my name is Jen,
I was an early employee at Lever, and

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:48.810
we build hiring software.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.370
So if you've applied for a job or
an internship in the last year or two,

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:53.790
you've probably used Lever before.

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:57.290
As for my role, I've had various roles
building and leading teams in our product,

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:00.381
customer success recruiting, and
employee experience functions.

00:17:00.381 --> 00:17:01.867
And we're over 100.

00:17:03.643 --> 00:17:06.047
&gt;&gt; And they're at 50/50 gender split.

00:17:06.047 --> 00:17:07.389
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Which is-

00:17:07.389 --> 00:17:07.987
&gt;&gt; Among other things.

00:17:07.987 --> 00:17:11.302
&gt;&gt; Yeah, among other things,
but it's hard to do.

00:17:11.302 --> 00:17:15.961
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Cristina Cordoba, and
I manage the partnerships team at Stripe.

00:17:15.961 --> 00:17:20.574
Stripe builds tools and
infrastructure to accept payments for

00:17:20.574 --> 00:17:24.500
online businesses, and
we are about 750 people.

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:27.775
And I also spend a significant amount
of my time on our diversity and

00:17:27.775 --> 00:17:29.072
inclusion initiatives.

00:17:29.072 --> 00:17:30.610
&gt;&gt; I think I have a mic here,
can you guys hear me?

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:36.800
All right, my name is Cat Perez, and I am
the co-founder and CPO at HealthSherpa.

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:39.770
We are a technology platform and
team of people,

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:43.520
helping individuals enroll
in marketplace coverage.

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:47.150
We've enrolled over 840,000
folks on our platform to date.

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:50.174
Apart from products and
just leadership things,

00:17:50.174 --> 00:17:52.409
I also lead up D&amp;I at HealthSherpa.

00:17:52.409 --> 00:17:56.286
And we're about 20, scaling up to
about 40, 50, in the on-season,

00:17:56.286 --> 00:17:57.787
the open enrollment season.

00:18:00.292 --> 00:18:04.183
&gt;&gt; Cool, my name is Makinde Adeagbo,
I'm the founder and CEO of dev/color.

00:18:04.183 --> 00:18:08.370
We help black software engineers set and
reach ambitious career goals.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:10.272
We are a team of three.

00:18:12.908 --> 00:18:16.662
&gt;&gt; My name is Mitchell Lee,
I'm one of the two co-founders of Penny.

00:18:16.662 --> 00:18:21.367
Which is a personal finance app, that you
interact with by way of conversation.

00:18:21.367 --> 00:18:24.572
We are a five person team.

00:18:24.572 --> 00:18:28.928
&gt;&gt; Awesome, so one of the things that was
very clear to me, as I talked to a lot of

00:18:28.928 --> 00:18:33.605
YC alumni, is that diversity is sort of
defined differently for every company.

00:18:33.605 --> 00:18:37.680
So I wanted to ask a couple of you,
why was D&amp;I important for you and

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.710
your company, and how did you define it?

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:40.841
Let's start with Jen.

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:45.560
&gt;&gt; So why was diversity and
inclusion important for us?

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:51.270
Even though we are an HR tech startup, it
really just started as very organically.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:54.198
What do we want to do as people and
as coworkers,

00:18:54.198 --> 00:18:56.918
wow can we build
an inclusive place to work?

00:18:56.918 --> 00:19:01.114
Actually, once we started telling stories,
writing blog posts about it, we realized

00:19:01.114 --> 00:19:04.950
there was a lot of resonating with
both our applicants and our customers.

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:08.581
They wanted to know, what are you doing
differently, what can we learn from?

00:19:08.581 --> 00:19:10.837
And realizing, as leaders in this space,

00:19:10.837 --> 00:19:14.295
we had a lot to do to show here's
what we're experimenting with.

00:19:14.295 --> 00:19:17.590
And sometimes, let's be real,
sometimes we make mistakes in.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:20.860
And to be honest, what's been really cool
is that it makes a lot of difference in

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:22.960
also sales and growth.

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:27.261
So that we're able to show,
this is how we're approaching culture and

00:19:27.261 --> 00:19:29.707
inclusion, with this much intention.

00:19:29.707 --> 00:19:32.454
It allows prospective
customers to look at, well.

00:19:32.454 --> 00:19:35.570
If they are building their
internal company culture that way,

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:37.822
I want to know what their
software looks like.

00:19:37.822 --> 00:19:39.360
I want to know what their product is like.

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.620
So it's been a really huge benefit for us,

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:43.440
that it gets to be
an ongoing conversation.

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:50.350
&gt;&gt; Cool, I wanted to, let's go with Kat.

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:54.575
I'd really liked hearing about why you
thought it was important, both for

00:19:54.575 --> 00:19:57.068
your business case, and for your culture.

00:19:57.068 --> 00:20:03.131
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, so for our team,
I mean, I was hire number seven.

00:20:03.131 --> 00:20:07.249
And we, I mean, we're a pretty small team,
we scaled up really quickly.

00:20:07.249 --> 00:20:12.379
But by nature of who we're serving,
we are serving folks of lower income.

00:20:12.379 --> 00:20:15.261
Folks who would qualify for
subsidies on the marketplace.

00:20:15.261 --> 00:20:19.027
Kaiser Family Foundation released
a report, I think, for 2015.

00:20:19.027 --> 00:20:23.511
The majority of
the uninsured-under-65 market in

00:20:23.511 --> 00:20:29.266
the United States are people of color,
over half, so 55 to 60%.

00:20:29.266 --> 00:20:32.401
Specifically Latinx and
Hispanic communities, as well as black and

00:20:32.401 --> 00:20:34.030
African-American communities.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:36.430
So, for us it was, it was,
it's a no-brainer, I mean,

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:39.130
our mission is to enroll these people.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:43.080
Give them access to coverage,
let them know what options they have.

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:47.160
You can't do that, if you have a team
that does not reflect that audience.

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:50.730
So for us, it was really simple and
straightforward, why we needed to do that.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:54.032
&gt;&gt; And I'd love to hear
a little bit about Stripe.

00:20:54.032 --> 00:21:00.854
Why it's important, how you've defined it,
how you interacted with founders as well.

00:21:00.854 --> 00:21:05.419
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I would say, on our side,
we thought about this really early.

00:21:05.419 --> 00:21:08.963
But probably not in the way that
you would think a company would.

00:21:08.963 --> 00:21:13.816
Both of our founders are Irish, and
immigrated to the United States.

00:21:13.816 --> 00:21:16.828
A lot of our really early
employees are also immigrants.

00:21:16.828 --> 00:21:20.846
Everywhere from Honduras to Kenya,
to Sweden,

00:21:20.846 --> 00:21:23.985
all in our first 20 or so employees.

00:21:23.985 --> 00:21:27.617
And I think one of the reasons for
that is because of our core mission.

00:21:27.617 --> 00:21:32.123
Which is really looking at, how can
we enable economic opportunities and

00:21:32.123 --> 00:21:36.860
access to creating an online business,
no matter where you are in the world?

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:40.789
We've launched products like Atlas, which
enable entrepreneurs around the world,

00:21:40.789 --> 00:21:43.069
to get set up with a US entity and
a US bank account.

00:21:43.069 --> 00:21:45.965
Which if you are an entrepreneur in Cuba,
or

00:21:45.965 --> 00:21:50.995
if you're an entrepreneur in Canada,
very difficult to do regardless.

00:21:50.995 --> 00:21:56.225
And I think for us early on, it was about,
how can we build this into our culture?

00:21:56.225 --> 00:21:59.585
Because we know that we need
to launch a product that works

00:21:59.585 --> 00:22:04.280
in 20-plus countries now,
hopefully every country eventually.

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:07.450
And we need to have people on board who
really understand, what are the right

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:10.640
products to be building for different
entrepreneurs, all around the world?

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:13.987
And how can we make it easier for
people to become entrepreneurs?

00:22:13.987 --> 00:22:18.370
The other piece really early on was,
in working on diversity and inclusion.

00:22:18.370 --> 00:22:22.013
Making sure that we had a direct line to,
ultimately, people who were

00:22:22.013 --> 00:22:25.995
making decisions that affect inclusion and
diversity across the company.

00:22:25.995 --> 00:22:30.789
So for us, that means me working very,
very closely with our CEO and COO.

00:22:30.789 --> 00:22:34.468
And meeting with them every other week on,
how are we on doing on these various

00:22:34.468 --> 00:22:38.510
different aspects of diversity and
inclusion, throughout the company?

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:41.650
And making sure that they
are aware of what's going on, and

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:45.430
that they are approving every single
thing, every step of the way.

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:47.490
And that way,
I can go about what I need to do,

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:48.830
to make things happen within the company.

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:53.310
&gt;&gt; So giving you that autonomy, and
that ability to actually make change?

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:55.458
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Where you see needed.

00:22:55.458 --> 00:23:00.972
So one, the next question I had is, when
did you first start thinking about D&amp;I?

00:23:00.972 --> 00:23:03.000
And a couple of you had some
interesting conversations.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:07.950
And so I want to talk to
Ashu about Make School, and

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:10.950
your experience sort of being
two guys starting this company.

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:13.550
When did the conversation first come up?

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:15.760
&gt;&gt; Sure, so
the conversation first came up, for us,

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:17.730
when we started talking
to some of our students.

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:19.210
At the first summer program that we had,

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:22.080
we had a particular female student,
Sophie.

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:25.801
Who, from mine and my co-founder's
perspective, was this confident,

00:23:25.801 --> 00:23:28.175
badass girl who was
building this amazing app.

00:23:28.175 --> 00:23:31.009
And we never really realized
she had a bit of a struggle,

00:23:31.009 --> 00:23:33.914
when she was actually going
through our summer program.

00:23:33.914 --> 00:23:36.537
And it wasn't until a year later,
where we started to talking to some of our

00:23:36.537 --> 00:23:38.843
students about what their experience
with our program was like.

00:23:38.843 --> 00:23:42.930
And where we really heard that, behind
the scenes, there were a lot of concerns

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:46.163
around how she fit into
the environment of the summer program.

00:23:46.163 --> 00:23:49.757
And its important to acknowledge that,
it's not that the environment was

00:23:49.757 --> 00:23:52.389
explicitly sexist, or
explicitly discriminatory.

00:23:52.389 --> 00:23:55.261
But there's just a lot, just in terms
of the culture and the language.

00:23:55.261 --> 00:23:57.200
That you might not obviously flag as,

00:23:57.200 --> 00:24:01.634
this is something that might make someone
feel uncomfortable, or feel not included.

00:24:01.634 --> 00:24:05.517
And it was only really hearing her
experiences that made us think one level

00:24:05.517 --> 00:24:06.025
deeper.

00:24:06.025 --> 00:24:10.323
Not just the surface-level challenge,
but one level deeper than that.

00:24:10.323 --> 00:24:12.245
How do we go above and beyond, to try and

00:24:12.245 --> 00:24:16.472
make this summer environment a lot more
inclusive for For people like Sophie, so

00:24:16.472 --> 00:24:19.269
it was really shocking to us
to hear about this experience.

00:24:19.269 --> 00:24:22.009
And because we thought that things
were actually pretty good and

00:24:22.009 --> 00:24:24.420
that we had a pretty good
culture in the program.

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:27.246
But it wasn't until we actually had
these kind of hard conversations.

00:24:27.246 --> 00:24:31.534
And got some very candid feedback to
realize we had a long way to go to improve

00:24:31.534 --> 00:24:33.030
that environment.

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:36.940
&gt;&gt; Can you give one example of something
that she experienced that you didn't

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:43.170
realize was giving her a hard time or
making her less able to perform well?

00:24:43.170 --> 00:24:45.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah, some of the things were
a little bit less gendered.

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:48.505
It was around,
she was someone who was very athletic and

00:24:48.505 --> 00:24:52.019
had a lot of hobbies around not just
computer science and app development.

00:24:52.019 --> 00:24:54.513
And most of the students were very
focused around app development and

00:24:54.513 --> 00:24:55.910
computers and video games.

00:24:55.910 --> 00:24:59.490
And as a result didn't necessarily feel
like she fit in socially as well with

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:00.940
a lot of the other students.

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:05.130
And a lot of it is also just feeling
whether like she had a place in

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:10.211
the environment when you step into a room
where you're one of the few girls.

00:25:10.211 --> 00:25:13.294
Or one of the few people of color it
becomes a lot tougher to feel like

00:25:13.294 --> 00:25:13.853
you fit in.

00:25:13.853 --> 00:25:17.940
And your doubt, whenever you're having
questions or struggles about I don't

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:21.430
know how to build this feature,
I don't know how to solve this bug.

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:23.857
There's a two questions that
are being asked is, one,

00:25:23.857 --> 00:25:25.415
is it just that I did not learn this.

00:25:25.415 --> 00:25:29.091
Or also is it because I do not fit here
because I am not a person who should be

00:25:29.091 --> 00:25:30.120
a programmer.

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:33.666
There is that kind of rhetoric that
society has kind of pushed especially at

00:25:33.666 --> 00:25:37.269
the high school level where you tend to
have a lot more boys who are in computer

00:25:37.269 --> 00:25:38.525
science or math classes.

00:25:38.525 --> 00:25:42.458
And that sort of second line of
questioning makes people and her case

00:25:42.458 --> 00:25:47.780
other students to make them less likely
to approach instructors and ask for help.

00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:50.522
They feel like I should know this and
should be going for help for

00:25:50.522 --> 00:25:54.054
this kind of topic being and so be more
proactive like offering help definitely.

00:25:54.054 --> 00:25:57.708
&gt;&gt; And one example that you
gave that I thought was really,

00:25:57.708 --> 00:26:01.140
like stuck with me when
she didn't know something.

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:04.091
You know some of the very young
men in the class, high schoolers,

00:26:04.091 --> 00:26:05.924
would be like you don't know that?

00:26:05.924 --> 00:26:10.410
And I think just like trying to like,
I guess like coach people through.

00:26:10.410 --> 00:26:14.208
Like here's an appropriate action
when someone asks a question.

00:26:14.208 --> 00:26:18.709
And I think like sharing a really small
personal side note on that is that for

00:26:18.709 --> 00:26:23.440
the first year for example at YC I didn't,
I used to wear dresses all the time.

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:28.387
And I didn't, I've wore jeans and a
graphic TV because I just want to fit in.

00:26:28.387 --> 00:26:31.788
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I realized I was using way more

00:26:31.788 --> 00:26:37.590
brain cycles to really try to fit in and
be taken seriously than I needed to be.

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:41.670
But I wish I'd had this
conversation because,

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:46.465
yes [LAUGH] I think that other
people felt the same thing.

00:26:46.465 --> 00:26:50.008
And I'd never talked about it until
actually right now, so there you go,

00:26:50.008 --> 00:26:51.535
sharing a story, first time.

00:26:51.535 --> 00:26:53.774
[LAUGH] Okay.

00:26:53.774 --> 00:26:56.831
And I would like to talk
to you Mitch about Penny,

00:26:56.831 --> 00:27:00.562
because you are one of the kind of you and
I think Dav Collar,

00:27:00.562 --> 00:27:03.936
probably the most recent
companies to go through YC.

00:27:03.936 --> 00:27:05.304
And this is something that
you've thought about a lot.

00:27:05.304 --> 00:27:07.368
So, why are you thinking about it?

00:27:07.368 --> 00:27:08.701
You're a team of five?

00:27:08.701 --> 00:27:12.131
&gt;&gt; Five.
[COUGH] So I guess I should address

00:27:12.131 --> 00:27:17.900
the elephant in the room which is
I am a white male on a DNI Panel.

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:23.030
Raised a round of funding for Penny and

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:26.468
started to look to expand our team
at the time we were just two people.

00:27:26.468 --> 00:27:29.740
And with a little bit of back story here,

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:35.170
Penny is a conversational app that
helps you with your finances.

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:39.655
It's really intended to help people who
struggle with financial literacy or

00:27:39.655 --> 00:27:41.733
just find finances intimidating.

00:27:41.733 --> 00:27:46.794
That correlates strongly with people
that don't make a lot of money,

00:27:46.794 --> 00:27:48.606
lower income people so.

00:27:48.606 --> 00:27:53.068
We were building this interface
which has this personality and

00:27:53.068 --> 00:27:58.440
I'm a prolific emoji user and so
Penny ended up using a lot of emojis.

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:03.181
And one day my fiance kind of tapped
me on the shoulder and she says,

00:28:03.181 --> 00:28:05.729
I think Penny's flirting with me.

00:28:05.729 --> 00:28:11.372
And I was like, whoa,
I just use emoji that's my style.

00:28:11.372 --> 00:28:13.820
But kind of experiences like that.

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:17.897
And I heard from other friends as well
really hammered home the point that if

00:28:17.897 --> 00:28:21.132
we want to build a product
that's accessible to everyone we

00:28:21.132 --> 00:28:25.099
need to have a team that looks like
the people that we're trying to help.

00:28:25.099 --> 00:28:28.656
And is kind of representative of the group
of people that we're trying to build for.

00:28:28.656 --> 00:28:33.556
So that was one of the turning moments for
why we need to focus on this right now.

00:28:33.556 --> 00:28:35.445
&gt;&gt; And, okay.

00:28:35.445 --> 00:28:39.404
And Makite you had mentioned,
and I mentioned it briefly that

00:28:39.404 --> 00:28:44.065
you think that inclusion is something
that you can tank really early on.

00:28:44.065 --> 00:28:48.843
So are there any example's you've seen,
either on the positive or negative

00:28:48.843 --> 00:28:53.560
side of companies that you've worked
with that are doing this really well?

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:56.611
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
there's a lot I could say on this one and

00:28:56.611 --> 00:29:00.534
I'll start with an example
just from my own organization.

00:29:00.534 --> 00:29:05.153
So clearly we're a diversity organization
so it matters a lot to us both for

00:29:05.153 --> 00:29:09.340
the team that we build but
also the membership that we build.

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:12.415
One of my big mistakes early
on was taking it for granted.

00:29:12.415 --> 00:29:17.595
So I'm a black man, so
I was like okay, that should be easy.

00:29:17.595 --> 00:29:19.023
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But

00:29:19.023 --> 00:29:22.708
I actually very quickly when starting the
organization fell into this trap of, okay,

00:29:22.708 --> 00:29:24.877
the initial members will
be the people that I know,

00:29:24.877 --> 00:29:27.170
because that's who it's
easiest to start with.

00:29:27.170 --> 00:29:31.262
Turns out there not a lot of black women
in software engineering in that initial

00:29:31.262 --> 00:29:33.315
set of people that I brought together.

00:29:33.315 --> 00:29:38.293
And right away it became a very
clear issue that the gender balance

00:29:38.293 --> 00:29:39.955
in our group was off.

00:29:39.955 --> 00:29:42.417
It's something that we
have worked on since then.

00:29:42.417 --> 00:29:47.242
It's still not where we still have a lot
and its something we keep an eye on.

00:29:47.242 --> 00:29:52.410
But even just realizing that even I had a
blind spot was a big wake up call for me.

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:56.930
So I'm guessing everyone has
a blind spot for different groups.

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:57.937
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:57.937 --> 00:30:00.890
&gt;&gt; So they're small signals
The other thing I'll say is,

00:30:00.890 --> 00:30:06.635
just piggybacking on people feeling
like they belong somewhere.

00:30:06.635 --> 00:30:10.941
That's another thing that I think is
really easy to mess up just realizing that

00:30:10.941 --> 00:30:14.989
you and your company are constantly
sending out signals of who belongs and

00:30:14.989 --> 00:30:15.854
who doesn't.

00:30:15.854 --> 00:30:21.245
So for us something as simple as sending
out our members to go on off-sites, right?

00:30:21.245 --> 00:30:24.325
Like, I was making a list of like,
here's some off-sites you can consider.

00:30:24.325 --> 00:30:27.815
And I just put a list of things that I
enjoyed which are like zip lining or

00:30:27.815 --> 00:30:29.600
rock climbing.

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:33.633
And for some people that was great, for
other people it sent a subtle signal again

00:30:33.633 --> 00:30:36.143
like, hey is this group for
me, do I belong here?

00:30:36.143 --> 00:30:39.390
So I had to actually go back to the
drawing board and thankfully we had a lot

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:42.860
of different people on our team who
could add different perspectives.

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:46.910
And also within the membership
who could help fill out that list

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:50.130
to be something that everyone would
find something that appealed to them.

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:52.212
&gt;&gt; That's really cool.

00:30:52.212 --> 00:30:57.902
And, so, one of the things that I think I
heard from all of you, and all the alumni

00:30:57.902 --> 00:31:02.929
talked about is one of the major kind
of obstacles to start implementing

00:31:02.929 --> 00:31:08.890
really impactful DNI programs is getting
people to have tough conversations.

00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:12.615
It's getting people, even if they're
uncomfortable to have, to share and

00:31:12.615 --> 00:31:14.002
have these conversations.

00:31:14.002 --> 00:31:19.109
So, and Jen can you talk a little bit
about you and what's going to come next

00:31:19.109 --> 00:31:24.569
is sort of inspired by what Jim told
about a pin, she heard about a Pinterest.

00:31:24.569 --> 00:31:29.785
But then also talk about how
you implemented that at lever.

00:31:29.785 --> 00:31:33.562
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the particular problem we were
trying to solve for at the time, about

00:31:33.562 --> 00:31:37.950
a year and a half ago was this question of
how do you make inclusion truly inclusive?

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:41.699
To make inclusion and culture building
a company-wide activity it's not just

00:31:41.699 --> 00:31:45.047
that's like the women's issue or
that's the racial minority's issue.

00:31:45.047 --> 00:31:49.758
So something we realized was when we had
events and different discussions about

00:31:49.758 --> 00:31:54.116
inclusion there was a smaller subset
of the company that was very active,

00:31:54.116 --> 00:31:55.880
very passionate about it.

00:31:55.880 --> 00:31:58.400
And actually a larger subset

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:01.540
that considered themselves kind of
a like a little bit on the sidelines.

00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:04.760
So don't get me wrong, they thought it was
a good idea, they were very supportive.

00:32:04.760 --> 00:32:10.190
But for whatever reason they were a little
bit hesitant to jump really deep in and

00:32:10.190 --> 00:32:11.740
be visible leaders for it.

00:32:11.740 --> 00:32:14.181
So I was kind of wrapping
this around my head.

00:32:14.181 --> 00:32:18.301
And I met a head of inclusion at
Pinterest, Candace at event and

00:32:18.301 --> 00:32:21.590
she told me about this
event that they had called.

00:32:22.870 --> 00:32:24.790
Sorry, I didn't mean to offend.

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:28.230
And what they did was put a members
of the majority group, I think,

00:32:28.230 --> 00:32:31.920
a lot of engineers, white males,
put them on a panel just like this.

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:36.790
And created a safe space where they
were able to talk about mistakes,

00:32:36.790 --> 00:32:40.010
missteps, here is something I just had
no idea about, I said, something and

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:42.140
I offended someone, and
I was so mortified.

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:48.108
But by having leaders and by having
their colleagues speak openly about

00:32:48.108 --> 00:32:51.926
these events really set a tone for
the culture, right?

00:32:51.926 --> 00:32:55.280
Saying, you are not going to be
vilified by saying the wrong thing.

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:59.610
We are going to assume best intentions and
really try to go from there as a team.

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:04.540
So, what we ended up creating, inspired by
that idea, was a session called DNI for

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:05.260
our supporters.

00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:10.176
It was an internal seminar, led by,
actually, somebody on our team who's also

00:33:10.176 --> 00:33:13.182
from a majority group,
a straight, white, male engineer.

00:33:13.182 --> 00:33:17.138
And the fact that maybe was led
by him had two different factors.

00:33:17.138 --> 00:33:22.090
So, one, maybe,
because when the person leading it

00:33:22.090 --> 00:33:26.170
it someone from the majority group people
are like, I'm way more curious about it.

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:27.100
Maybe I should go,

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:30.570
even though previous events they
didn't seem like they were for me.

00:33:30.570 --> 00:33:35.610
And then second, again, really the
designing it with that audience in mind.

00:33:35.610 --> 00:33:39.810
The subtitle to that seminar was DNI for
Dummies.

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:43.550
So, it's like if you were ever confused,
or uncertain, and

00:33:43.550 --> 00:33:45.090
you just didn't want to
say the wrong thing, so

00:33:45.090 --> 00:33:49.540
you've been holding yourself back,
this is the space to come for you.

00:33:49.540 --> 00:33:51.210
And it ended up being really successful.

00:33:51.210 --> 00:33:54.470
And a lot of people who previously
weren't actively involved came,

00:33:54.470 --> 00:33:57.560
and shared really personal stories,
and opened up.

00:33:57.560 --> 00:34:02.080
And just to wrap this up, I think,
something one of my coworkers had said.

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:07.830
So, this was a black male coworker of
mine who said, you know, when the cause

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:12.510
isn't your own, it can feel really
uncomfortable to speak out on behalf.

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:14.540
Even if you really,
really want to, because, again,

00:34:14.540 --> 00:34:19.370
that fear of saying the wrong thing,
being misinterpreted, being attacked even.

00:34:19.370 --> 00:34:23.620
So, it really is a deliberate effort you
have to make for your future employees,

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:25.505
your culture, to say that this is okay.

00:34:25.505 --> 00:34:27.784
We're going to navigate
through these things,

00:34:27.784 --> 00:34:30.938
just like you would with any
business problem for a company.

00:34:30.938 --> 00:34:36.500
&gt;&gt; So, in the spirit of getting
people talk about hard things and

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:37.510
having hard conversations,

00:34:37.510 --> 00:34:41.680
I would like everyone on this panel to
share a story about mistake they made.

00:34:41.680 --> 00:34:46.526
Or a misstep, something that they
wish they could have a do-over on

00:34:46.526 --> 00:34:51.032
when they started thinking about DNI,
at their workplace, so

00:34:51.032 --> 00:34:53.426
let's talk about our mistakes.

00:34:53.426 --> 00:34:54.341
Do you want to start?

00:34:54.341 --> 00:34:59.660
[LAUGH] You can volunteer.

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:02.671
&gt;&gt; I think, I went already, so.

00:35:02.671 --> 00:35:03.620
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:08.210
I messed up on gender balance for
the organization at the start, and

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:13.650
I'm still developing more strategies
to sustainably fix that issue.

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:23.612
&gt;&gt; So, I've made a lot of mistakes.

00:35:23.612 --> 00:35:25.360
I will say, that we're pretty early on.

00:35:25.360 --> 00:35:26.890
And so, for

00:35:26.890 --> 00:35:31.280
us, I don't view it so much as mistakes,
as just kind of learning opportunities.

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:35.150
There was a lot of learning opportunities
as we started this process.

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:39.400
How do you approach the subject
with people that you're

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:41.080
interested in recruiting.

00:35:41.080 --> 00:35:45.510
How do you position it within your own
company, and have an open dialogue there.

00:35:46.850 --> 00:35:51.852
There were also just
small missteps like we

00:35:51.852 --> 00:35:55.080
had one woman that actually
ended up joining our team.

00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:57.220
We had a dinner with the team.

00:35:57.220 --> 00:36:00.790
And the way that we were positioned at
the dinner was just very aggressive,

00:36:00.790 --> 00:36:02.700
it was like a noisy environment.

00:36:02.700 --> 00:36:06.460
She was surrounded by men, and
we were peppering her with questions and

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:09.940
that's just not a way to make
somebody feel comfortable.

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:14.040
And open up with you kind of
like on a personal level.

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:16.631
There are a lot.

00:36:16.631 --> 00:36:18.319
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But, I do think,

00:36:18.319 --> 00:36:21.350
that we're learning and
we're getting better at this.

00:36:21.350 --> 00:36:23.568
Step one is just talk about it openly.

00:36:23.568 --> 00:36:31.425
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so, [LAUGH]
we had a few missteps,

00:36:31.425 --> 00:36:37.310
I'd like to call them, early on when
we started our DNI initiatives.

00:36:37.310 --> 00:36:41.350
We started about two years ago, and at the
time I had come on as hire number seven.

00:36:41.350 --> 00:36:43.060
So, we were still pretty small.

00:36:43.060 --> 00:36:48.120
Made the really big mistake of
assuming everyone had the same

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:51.660
perception of what diversity and
inclusion meant, to each and

00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:54.430
every one of us, and
our lived experiences.

00:36:54.430 --> 00:36:58.438
And that means everyone,
including white, cis-males on the team.

00:36:58.438 --> 00:37:01.270
All right, so, Jenny talked about this,

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:05.230
but one of our biggest missteps
was just not necessarily even

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:10.850
practicing this concept of inclusion with
our white cis-male team members, right?

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:15.889
Their thoughts matter, and they need to
be involved in the conversation as well.

00:37:15.889 --> 00:37:20.144
So, I think, that was our biggest mistake,
was just assuming,

00:37:20.144 --> 00:37:24.186
team of seven everyone conceptually
agrees we should do this, but

00:37:24.186 --> 00:37:28.950
not really having that tough uncomfortable
[LAUGH] conversation about it, and

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:32.290
then the second thing
I just said there is.

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:37.320
We approach it as a to-do list,
which is a terrible thing to do.

00:37:37.320 --> 00:37:39.230
We [LAUGH] were overly ambitious.

00:37:39.230 --> 00:37:42.230
We had like seven initiatives we
wanted to roll out at the same time.

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:44.920
Definitely don't do that.

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:47.080
[LAUGH] One is enough.

00:37:47.080 --> 00:37:52.155
We learned to approach MVP style,
start small, because this stuff

00:37:52.155 --> 00:37:58.211
is really emotionally taxing, and
intense, and triggering for people.

00:37:58.211 --> 00:38:02.013
So, yeah, just starting small would have
been something we would have changed from

00:38:02.013 --> 00:38:02.555
the start.

00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:08.012
I can go, yeah.

00:38:08.012 --> 00:38:11.320
So, I would say for us at Stripe.

00:38:11.320 --> 00:38:15.190
Probably one of the biggest mistakes
we made fairly early on was,

00:38:15.190 --> 00:38:19.290
we need to hire a pretty significant
number of engineers in a very

00:38:19.290 --> 00:38:22.940
fast [LAUGH] period of
time in our early growth.

00:38:24.220 --> 00:38:29.360
And we hadn't necessary hired the number
of engineers that we were looking to hire

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:30.430
in that period of time,

00:38:30.430 --> 00:38:35.040
which was about three months,
at that rate ever in our history.

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:39.780
And we set a really like audacious goal,
in terms of like the percentage of women

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:43.580
and percentage of under represented
minorities, that would make up this

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:47.360
kind of batch of engineers that we wanted
to bring on in that short period of time.

00:38:47.360 --> 00:38:51.270
And so, we set this lofty goal,
we had all these aspirations, but

00:38:51.270 --> 00:38:54.610
we actually had no plan for
how to meet that goal.

00:38:54.610 --> 00:38:58.406
So, of course, we completely failed.

00:38:58.406 --> 00:39:01.901
And it was a very hard time,
I think, in the company,

00:39:01.901 --> 00:39:06.800
because we effectively hired all
the engineers that we wanted to hire, but

00:39:06.800 --> 00:39:11.073
not many of them were people of color,
or women, and as a result,

00:39:11.073 --> 00:39:15.543
we had to sit down as a company and
say, like, yeah, we screwed up.

00:39:15.543 --> 00:39:19.442
And we had to admit that, like add in all
hands, in front of our entire company, and

00:39:19.442 --> 00:39:23.150
start thinking about, okay, well, what
are we going to do to fix this, right?

00:39:23.150 --> 00:39:27.160
Because we are going to continue to hire
engineers, and we need to set up a plan,

00:39:27.160 --> 00:39:28.970
so that we can do that
in a more effective way.

00:39:28.970 --> 00:39:35.050
So, what we did from that point on was,
say, that, I think, at Stripe at the time,

00:39:35.050 --> 00:39:39.020
we had this kind of disconnect between
recruiting and hiring managers.

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:41.940
So, I think, in a lot of ways,
hiring managers,

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:45.890
people who need to add engineers on to
their team think about in the context of

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:48.540
recruiting is bringing
me these candidates.

00:39:48.540 --> 00:39:53.640
And so, I'm going to find the best ones
and bring them through my hiring pipeline.

00:39:53.640 --> 00:39:58.050
But the reality is that as a hiring
manager it's your responsibility for

00:39:58.050 --> 00:40:00.520
what your team looks like
at the end of the day.

00:40:00.520 --> 00:40:03.990
And you need to take an active
role in that hiring process.

00:40:03.990 --> 00:40:06.710
So, we actually implemented
two quarters ago,

00:40:06.710 --> 00:40:11.680
a goal effectively saying that
every engineering manager.

00:40:11.680 --> 00:40:16.250
Is required to bring an underrepresented
candidate, so a person of color or

00:40:16.250 --> 00:40:17.800
a woman, for engineering.

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:23.200
All the way through to the on site
stage of our hiring pipeline at Stripe.

00:40:23.200 --> 00:40:29.660
And this really put the onus on the eng
managers to do this because we, one,

00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:34.350
created a internally public spreadsheet in
the company of all the eng managers and

00:40:34.350 --> 00:40:36.920
which one of them had met the goal.

00:40:36.920 --> 00:40:40.770
And so, they could see this,
their team members could see this.

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:44.260
And so, if they weren't dedicating
the time necessary to meet this goal,

00:40:44.260 --> 00:40:45.790
everyone would know about it.

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:50.140
And so, that really put the power in the
hands of the higher managers to say, well,

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:54.030
okay, like how can I go out there and meet
people who are not in my direct network?

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:58.270
And how can I insure that we are bringing
more people from different backgrounds

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:01.436
into the funnel of our hiring pipeline.

00:41:01.436 --> 00:41:03.973
And we three exed the number
of women in engineering

00:41:03.973 --> 00:41:07.749
that we would normally hire during that
period of time through that effort.

00:41:10.291 --> 00:41:13.950
&gt;&gt; Cool, I have a story
about how we made a mistake.

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:17.170
And really a valuable lesson was,
how we were going to treat and

00:41:17.170 --> 00:41:19.740
kind of deal with other
future problems like that.

00:41:19.740 --> 00:41:23.340
So we have an employer resource
group called Leverettes.

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:26.070
And that's what we call
female employees of Lever.

00:41:26.070 --> 00:41:29.580
It's a slack channel and dinner like,
I think two or three times a year,

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:32.790
to talk about issues,
you know resources, mentorship.

00:41:34.230 --> 00:41:36.750
I had found out that about a year and
a half ago,

00:41:36.750 --> 00:41:39.996
somebody had created a private
Slack channel Lever Dudes.

00:41:39.996 --> 00:41:44.455
And so, this is a little bit of gray area,
and I think you'll find the pattern,

00:41:44.455 --> 00:41:48.650
in that, the gray areas and the kind of
like the awkward, uncomfortable and

00:41:48.650 --> 00:41:51.200
ambiguous problems
are some of the hardest.

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:55.600
Because, if you looked at the channel,
there was nothing super wrong with it.

00:41:55.600 --> 00:42:00.320
But there was this tone that could be
interpreted a little bit of a mocking.

00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:04.430
So, it was said something like, well,
they have a Leverettes channel, so

00:42:04.430 --> 00:42:05.040
it's only fair.

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:09.310
And there were some jokes that, again,
nothing super crazy but it didn't make

00:42:09.310 --> 00:42:12.970
people who were in it actually,
other men, didn't feel good about it.

00:42:12.970 --> 00:42:16.750
So when it was brought up to me,
we talk about how one of our standards and

00:42:16.750 --> 00:42:25.140
principles, for Dee and I, is this phrase,
if you see something, say something.

00:42:25.140 --> 00:42:27.730
The standard that you walk past
is a standard that you accept.

00:42:28.730 --> 00:42:31.700
And even though it's comfortable,
it was our job,

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:34.900
as start up employees, as leaders,
to do something about it.

00:42:34.900 --> 00:42:39.670
So we reached out to one of our founders,
Randall, who was incredibly empathetic,

00:42:39.670 --> 00:42:42.600
listened to the situation,
took it really seriously.

00:42:42.600 --> 00:42:43.800
The problem here was that,

00:42:43.800 --> 00:42:48.810
again, it was undermining the other
Leverettes channel and the group.

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:51.650
And also, if left alone,
it could potentially lead to a little

00:42:51.650 --> 00:42:54.320
bit of a us versus them mentality,
if it were to grow.

00:42:55.340 --> 00:43:00.420
So he ended up addressing the employee
in question, super professionally,

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:03.610
not trying to call him out, just saying
that like hey, this is not okay.

00:43:03.610 --> 00:43:05.710
So I'll like to talk about what happened.

00:43:05.710 --> 00:43:09.590
Again, really try to understand and
listen, but then also draw the lines.

00:43:09.590 --> 00:43:13.800
And then, I think the next part, what he
did, was really something up as a leader.

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:18.390
And again, like Stripe, we decided
to address it at our next all hands.

00:43:18.390 --> 00:43:20.040
And I think that's one
of our key learnings.

00:43:20.040 --> 00:43:22.040
You have to address things.

00:43:22.040 --> 00:43:22.780
It's not fun.

00:43:22.780 --> 00:43:26.249
Nobody goes up to all hands in front of
your entire company saying, this happened

00:43:26.249 --> 00:43:29.120
and it was super uncomfortable and
awkward, but you have to do it.

00:43:29.120 --> 00:43:31.190
Otherwise, it becomes
an elephant in the room.

00:43:31.190 --> 00:43:33.908
So Randall addressed the entire company,
and

00:43:33.908 --> 00:43:37.531
what was really interesting was
he started the story saying,

00:43:37.531 --> 00:43:41.480
by being very honest about a mistake
that he had made in the past.

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:45.450
So, when the Leverettes group was first
formed, and it was just the four of us

00:43:45.450 --> 00:43:49.600
women just going out to dinner, he made a
joke about like well maybe I'll grab some

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:53.040
of the engineers and go and get pizza
dough or something, like the same night.

00:43:53.040 --> 00:43:57.721
And it wasn't until later he realized
that wasn't what he meant to say,

00:43:57.721 --> 00:44:02.786
because, again, if you think about why
that group exists in the first place,

00:44:02.786 --> 00:44:07.414
because women may not be minorities,
but they are minorities in tech.

00:44:07.414 --> 00:44:10.232
And, therefore,
they have experiences, burdens,

00:44:10.232 --> 00:44:12.630
hardships that you might
not even know about.

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:17.430
So the reason the group exists is
not to have fun and go to drinks,

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:19.840
and talk about, I don't know, nail polish.

00:44:19.840 --> 00:44:24.510
It really is about the work, and
what can we do to support ourselves,

00:44:24.510 --> 00:44:29.040
each other, and just for
future employees and future inclusion.

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:32.200
And just being really honest and
authentic as a leader, I think,

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:33.810
made a huge difference.

00:44:33.810 --> 00:44:35.310
Later that day in Slack,

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:40.510
multiple employees both, and also in
person, went to Randall to thank him.

00:44:40.510 --> 00:44:41.487
To say things like,

00:44:41.487 --> 00:44:44.650
my previous start up founders
would never do anything like that.

00:44:44.650 --> 00:44:47.570
I feel really grateful and
lucky to work for place where the leaders

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:50.430
really care about the people, and
it's really human and authentic.

00:44:50.430 --> 00:44:53.280
So I think that was
a lesson that we leaned.

00:44:53.280 --> 00:44:55.170
It's not just to address it one or

00:44:55.170 --> 00:44:58.340
one, you want to prevent it
from happening in the future.

00:44:58.340 --> 00:45:01.470
Very easily, could a new hire could
have been drawing six months later and

00:45:01.470 --> 00:45:02.870
the same thing happens over again.

00:45:02.870 --> 00:45:06.640
So how do you address head on,
really step up as a leader.

00:45:06.640 --> 00:45:09.510
It's not going to be fun, sometimes,
it's not going to be comfortable, but

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:11.480
that is your responsibility.

00:45:11.480 --> 00:45:13.093
But the thing is, people will remember.

00:45:13.093 --> 00:45:16.387
A tone you said, what you say,
your employees remember it, and

00:45:16.387 --> 00:45:18.766
it gets embedded into
the culture as you grow.

00:45:21.310 --> 00:45:22.980
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so two quick ones.

00:45:22.980 --> 00:45:26.090
One Kat touched on earlier,
which is the concept of feigning surprise.

00:45:26.090 --> 00:45:28.340
So in the first summer,
we had asked certain students to,

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:30.970
hey, can you zip up your folder and
email it to us?

00:45:30.970 --> 00:45:33.080
And students would say, hey,
I don't know how to zip up a folder.

00:45:33.080 --> 00:45:35.080
And we would say wait,
you don't know how to zip up a folder?

00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:36.560
That's, like, such a basic thing.

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:39.120
But it operates on this assumption that,

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:42.420
this person has gone through the same
education and upbringing that you have,

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:45.310
such that they'll actually know this
thing that you're asking them to do.

00:45:45.310 --> 00:45:48.760
So that was a big one,
especially from a standpoint of a teacher.

00:45:48.760 --> 00:45:50.510
Not a really great thing for
a student to hear.

00:45:50.510 --> 00:45:53.400
Second one was,
starting from early in the company,

00:45:53.400 --> 00:45:57.780
we had a very strong voice
culture in meetings that we had.

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:00.295
So, anytime there was some sort of
discussion about certain topics,

00:46:00.295 --> 00:46:03.140
it'd get pretty heated,
voices would raise.

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:07.140
Not in an argumentative or
healthy debate way.

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:10.853
But, it also means that people who are
more introverted or are more soft spoken,

00:46:10.853 --> 00:46:12.558
suddenly don't have as much voice.

00:46:12.558 --> 00:46:15.518
And it's really a big problem when you're
including those folks in meetings and

00:46:15.518 --> 00:46:17.550
they're really not getting
a chance to speak.

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:19.860
And those are really two
big mistakes that we made.

00:46:19.860 --> 00:46:21.280
And the main thing that we've done,

00:46:21.280 --> 00:46:25.120
from a cultural standpoint,
to help people work on these mistakes,

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:30.010
is really assuming negligence and
not malice in these kind of situations.

00:46:30.010 --> 00:46:33.020
And really focusing on
where people are going,

00:46:33.020 --> 00:46:34.808
as opposed to necessarily where they are.

00:46:34.808 --> 00:46:38.160
Both my cofounder and I were quite
young when starting the company so

00:46:38.160 --> 00:46:40.940
had lots of mistakes in terms
of leadership and management.

00:46:40.940 --> 00:46:43.960
And it's only because we have a culture
where other folks on the team are willing

00:46:43.960 --> 00:46:47.030
to understand that we're
constantly trying to improve.

00:46:47.030 --> 00:46:50.940
And similarly, if you notice some
behavior and you say something about it,

00:46:50.940 --> 00:46:54.360
it's important not to make the person
feel that they are a bad person for

00:46:54.360 --> 00:46:55.180
having said this.

00:46:55.180 --> 00:46:57.630
But really just help coach them so
that, a year from now,

00:46:57.630 --> 00:47:00.620
they'll be a lot better at
this kind of situations.

00:47:00.620 --> 00:47:04.532
&gt;&gt; Cool, and you've all talked a little
bit about some things you're doing that

00:47:04.532 --> 00:47:05.548
are are going well.

00:47:05.548 --> 00:47:09.373
But shifting a little bit from the
mistakes that you've made in the past or

00:47:09.373 --> 00:47:12.025
that your organization
have made in the past.

00:47:12.025 --> 00:47:15.368
Let's start talking about
things that you're trying,

00:47:15.368 --> 00:47:20.171
that you're excited about, that are paying
off, that you think are going well,

00:47:20.171 --> 00:47:23.390
in terms of building this
great culture from scratch.

00:47:26.246 --> 00:47:27.482
&gt;&gt; Is it going well for anyone?

00:47:27.482 --> 00:47:29.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And yeah, and

00:47:29.650 --> 00:47:33.690
I think the thing is is like,
we know there's no easy button, but

00:47:33.690 --> 00:47:36.845
if there are any lights
that we can follow.

00:47:36.845 --> 00:47:37.770
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:47:37.770 --> 00:47:40.180
can only share what's worked for us.

00:47:40.180 --> 00:47:42.530
What's worked for
us may not work for everyone.

00:47:42.530 --> 00:47:47.970
There is no playbook, one size, or
blueprint that, one size fits all.

00:47:47.970 --> 00:47:54.120
For us, at Health Triple, we did, when we
were hiring, is we leveraged pot tools.

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:56.550
Not to be mistaken, but the other pot.

00:47:57.770 --> 00:48:01.040
So people ops tools,
that's what it stands for.

00:48:01.040 --> 00:48:05.550
One specifically that we used, it was
called Unidive, it's a Kapor Capital,

00:48:05.550 --> 00:48:08.620
a portfolio company,
they're called Talent Sonar now.

00:48:08.620 --> 00:48:11.670
And we use it to run our job
descriptions through it.

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:14.890
And the job description check
would score the job description,

00:48:14.890 --> 00:48:17.530
it would call out words
that were heavily biased.

00:48:17.530 --> 00:48:21.090
And the reason we did that was
to obviously cast a wider net.

00:48:21.090 --> 00:48:23.380
So super easy tool to use.

00:48:23.380 --> 00:48:24.740
They had other features,

00:48:24.740 --> 00:48:29.300
like the blind resume review,
as well as scoring questions.

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:33.610
And making sure that each person who
was interviewing a candidate was asking

00:48:33.610 --> 00:48:35.680
every single person the same question.

00:48:35.680 --> 00:48:38.320
And those questions mapped
back to competencies and

00:48:38.320 --> 00:48:39.410
skills that you need, right?

00:48:39.410 --> 00:48:43.732
So when you're a startup, you're
going to do things in a scrappy way,

00:48:43.732 --> 00:48:48.040
but we finally built some structure
around our interview process.

00:48:48.040 --> 00:48:52.235
And what we ended up finding after
rolling out that one initiative is,

00:48:52.235 --> 00:48:54.734
we ended up hitting our diversity goals.

00:48:54.734 --> 00:48:59.215
We 2 X-ed our Hispanic,
Latinx hires and team members,

00:48:59.215 --> 00:49:02.570
as well as our black team members as well.

00:49:02.570 --> 00:49:04.340
So it totally worked.

00:49:04.340 --> 00:49:08.120
Another thing I like to throw in there,
as we had that new

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:12.410
team leveraging that initiative, is we
had a plus 85% revenue growth as well.

00:49:13.890 --> 00:49:18.670
So I would like to attribute that
to the team that we had in place.

00:49:18.670 --> 00:49:23.942
So it's just a testament to the fact that
hiring diverse team members, especially

00:49:23.942 --> 00:49:29.004
when you are serving diverse communities
really does impact your bottom line.

00:49:31.035 --> 00:49:34.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I usually get this question a lot
because of what our organization is.

00:49:34.940 --> 00:49:38.340
A lot of founders of small companies
will come up and ask, hey,

00:49:38.340 --> 00:49:43.812
how do I hire more Latinx or
black or.female engineers?

00:49:43.812 --> 00:49:46.430
And what I usually actually tell them is,
look,

00:49:46.430 --> 00:49:51.270
if your company is four, five, ten people,
what you should really think about is,

00:49:51.270 --> 00:49:54.160
what are the attributes and
skills that you need on the team?

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:55.775
And be really honest about those things.

00:49:55.775 --> 00:49:59.340
So for example, if your building
an ag tech company, right,

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:04.074
and all of your employees are from cities,
that may not be the most effective thing

00:50:04.074 --> 00:50:07.727
when you're trying to sell, and
no one has ever been on a farm.

00:50:07.727 --> 00:50:09.527
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:50:09.527 --> 00:50:11.077
&gt;&gt; So I think it's step one for

00:50:11.077 --> 00:50:15.328
me is really taking a really honest look
at what you need your employees to be

00:50:15.328 --> 00:50:16.340
able to do.

00:50:16.340 --> 00:50:21.250
And what specific things matter to
you at the stage that you're at, and

00:50:21.250 --> 00:50:23.100
then hiring against those things.

00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:25.655
As opposed to just trying
to check the boxes,

00:50:25.655 --> 00:50:27.865
or make your numbers look a certain way.

00:50:27.865 --> 00:50:32.825
Because you know when you're larger,
the press will be looking at your stats.

00:50:32.825 --> 00:50:36.867
Figure out what's important to you at
your stage, and then hire against that.

00:50:40.464 --> 00:50:44.657
&gt;&gt; So I think the thing we probably
have done best is start early.

00:50:44.657 --> 00:50:49.007
We started thinking about this
when we were two and three people.

00:50:49.007 --> 00:50:52.242
That's important both because the people
you hire early on are eventually

00:50:52.242 --> 00:50:55.927
going to go on to be the leaders at
your company and hire their own teams.

00:50:55.927 --> 00:50:59.676
And so you want to make sure that you're
building the team now that you eventually

00:50:59.676 --> 00:51:00.440
want to have.

00:51:00.440 --> 00:51:05.360
It's also important because if you
end up as a team of 15 white and

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:07.590
Asian dudes sitting in a room and

00:51:07.590 --> 00:51:12.630
start trying to incorporate diversity
into your team at that point, good luck.

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:20.850
Because people don't want to be
a a token diversity candidate, or

00:51:20.850 --> 00:51:26.020
feel alienated by a culture that's
already in place, so that's a big one.

00:51:26.020 --> 00:51:27.760
We've also been pretty proactive.

00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:33.590
So you mentioned kind
of blinding the resume,

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:36.840
or putting together good job descriptions.

00:51:36.840 --> 00:51:42.310
That, at a very early stage,
is not quite enough,

00:51:42.310 --> 00:51:45.860
because you're working with
a much smaller funnel.

00:51:45.860 --> 00:51:48.410
So we've been very proactive about saying,

00:51:48.410 --> 00:51:52.100
no we're actually seeking out
people of different backgrounds and

00:51:52.100 --> 00:51:56.350
different cultures to try to incorporate
them into the team early on.

00:51:56.350 --> 00:52:01.234
And so partnering with different
organizations that can

00:52:01.234 --> 00:52:05.710
kind of help broaden your
recruiting funnel there,

00:52:05.710 --> 00:52:09.790
was something that I think
we did well early on.

00:52:13.561 --> 00:52:17.771
&gt;&gt; So for us, talking about these issues,
and whether it's community, culture,

00:52:17.771 --> 00:52:21.570
diversity, and talking about them
at effectively reset points.

00:52:21.570 --> 00:52:22.965
So for our education programs,

00:52:22.965 --> 00:52:25.740
when the programs actually
start is a really good time.

00:52:25.740 --> 00:52:29.370
But with the company, you can't always
go back and reset to the very beginning.

00:52:29.370 --> 00:52:32.780
So at a company off site, let us say in
January right after everyone has come back

00:52:32.780 --> 00:52:36.000
from winter break, usually is a good
time to address these kinds of topics.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:39.370
Because whatever impression you set and
whatever culture you start building

00:52:39.370 --> 00:52:43.390
in the first few weeks of the new year
will typically last throughout the year.

00:52:43.390 --> 00:52:47.109
And the second piece is providing
mentorship, especially for our students,

00:52:47.109 --> 00:52:50.616
and providing mentorship of people who
look like them and feel like them.

00:52:50.616 --> 00:52:53.660
We have one of our alums,
Leslie, she works at Lever.

00:52:53.660 --> 00:52:56.240
She's been amazing in terms of
creating a support group for

00:52:56.240 --> 00:52:58.340
our current female students
in our college program.

00:52:58.340 --> 00:53:01.160
That's gone a really
long way to helping that.

00:53:01.160 --> 00:53:03.240
And finally,
just finally talking about the numbers.

00:53:03.240 --> 00:53:07.490
You shouldn't be ashamed if your
numbers are not very diverse.

00:53:07.490 --> 00:53:09.990
Last year,
the first year of our college program,

00:53:09.990 --> 00:53:13.960
we had 1 girl out of about 22 students,
which was not great.

00:53:13.960 --> 00:53:15.330
But we still talked about that, and

00:53:15.330 --> 00:53:17.370
we thought about how can
we actively improve that.

00:53:17.370 --> 00:53:20.560
And because it was an open dialogue,

00:53:20.560 --> 00:53:22.570
we could think about how to
make plans to address that.

00:53:22.570 --> 00:53:27.725
And now we have about 7 girls out of 40,
or 36 students and

00:53:27.725 --> 00:53:30.920
100% retention throughout the year,
which is a big improvement from last year.

00:53:30.920 --> 00:53:33.161
And so for both programs as well as for
the staff,

00:53:33.161 --> 00:53:36.497
we've started talking about the numbers
when they weren't great, and

00:53:36.497 --> 00:53:39.298
then continued to increase and
improve them year over year.

00:53:40.802 --> 00:53:41.560
&gt;&gt; How are we doing on time?

00:53:42.570 --> 00:53:44.010
&gt;&gt; If you could each take one minute.

00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:47.670
And then I think I'll, yeah [LAUGH] sorry.

00:53:47.670 --> 00:53:49.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'll keep it short then.

00:53:49.220 --> 00:53:52.270
So you're hearing a lot of answers
to both on the diversity side and

00:53:52.270 --> 00:53:53.270
the inclusion side.

00:53:53.270 --> 00:53:58.072
And I can talk a lot about there, but one
thing that I think adding on to what those

00:53:58.072 --> 00:54:01.870
two at the end said,
really figure out what works for

00:54:01.870 --> 00:54:05.040
your company that might not be for
every other company.

00:54:05.040 --> 00:54:07.130
For us, we are a SaaS organization,

00:54:07.130 --> 00:54:11.230
in that we have quite a large
sales part of the company.

00:54:11.230 --> 00:54:15.085
And one of the very common associations
with what makes a really good salesperson

00:54:15.085 --> 00:54:17.770
is we should get some athletes in here,
right?

00:54:17.770 --> 00:54:20.220
Right, athletes are the best people.

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:23.360
And I have no doubt that some of
the best salespeople are, in fact,

00:54:23.360 --> 00:54:24.650
previous athletes.

00:54:24.650 --> 00:54:27.780
But that doesn't mean that if you
weren't an athlete you're going to be

00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:29.000
not good enough, right?

00:54:29.000 --> 00:54:33.340
So really took to examining some of
these biases and beliefs that we have in

00:54:33.340 --> 00:54:37.790
the Valley and work places in general and
really looking at, is that actually true?

00:54:37.790 --> 00:54:39.734
And is not going to work for
us at that Lever?

00:54:39.734 --> 00:54:44.283
So we've been able to have, yeah,
just make the previous college

00:54:44.283 --> 00:54:49.170
athletic experience required,
taking that out of job descriptions.

00:54:49.170 --> 00:54:52.591
And making sure people are not being
punished or feeling like they're being

00:54:52.591 --> 00:54:55.378
left out because they didn't
have this certain background.

00:54:58.152 --> 00:55:00.034
&gt;&gt; Last thing I'll say is,

00:55:00.034 --> 00:55:06.050
we did probably 20 things to improve our
intern diversity for this past year.

00:55:06.050 --> 00:55:11.860
So in 2016 we had 11% women and
underrepresented

00:55:11.860 --> 00:55:15.330
minorities in our internship class,
which was failure, mistake.

00:55:15.330 --> 00:55:20.240
And this year, we had over 41% women and
underrepresented minorities in our intern

00:55:20.240 --> 00:55:23.080
class, and
we over doubled the intern program.

00:55:23.080 --> 00:55:28.600
And I would say one of the things that we
did in particular, that had that effect,

00:55:28.600 --> 00:55:32.310
was we created multiple ways to
actually get into our intern pipeline.

00:55:32.310 --> 00:55:36.100
So your traditional way of showing
up at a career fair, for example,

00:55:36.100 --> 00:55:39.380
or applying to our website were still
ways that we looked at candidates.

00:55:39.380 --> 00:55:42.420
But we found that all the other technology
companies were looking at those

00:55:42.420 --> 00:55:43.340
exact same candidates.

00:55:43.340 --> 00:55:46.170
And you were looking at the same
candidates that had two other great

00:55:46.170 --> 00:55:47.920
internships at Google and Facebook, etc.

00:55:47.920 --> 00:55:50.530
And you were all fighting over
those particular candidates.

00:55:50.530 --> 00:55:54.990
When the reality is, you're looking for
capabilities at the internships stage,

00:55:54.990 --> 00:55:58.010
you're not necessarily looking for
people who have tons of experience.

00:55:58.010 --> 00:55:58.910
Because likely hood is,

00:55:58.910 --> 00:56:01.388
if you're in college, you probably
don't have very much experience at all.

00:56:01.388 --> 00:56:06.080
So, how could we hunt for people who have
those fantastic capabilities that we need,

00:56:06.080 --> 00:56:09.310
that we think are going to
do really well at Stripe?

00:56:09.310 --> 00:56:13.716
And so, one of the things we did was
create two additional application paths.

00:56:13.716 --> 00:56:18.315
One was a coding challenge that you
could do in your own free time and

00:56:18.315 --> 00:56:19.655
then submit that challenge to us.

00:56:19.655 --> 00:56:21.385
And you'd actually go straight
to a phone interview,

00:56:21.385 --> 00:56:23.605
you didn't even have to talk a recruiter.

00:56:23.605 --> 00:56:27.135
And then the other path was
a show us your code option.

00:56:27.135 --> 00:56:31.309
So we went to a bunch of events, like
the Black Society of Engineers at certain

00:56:31.309 --> 00:56:33.896
different colleges, the Grace Hopper, etc.

00:56:33.896 --> 00:56:36.921
And we said, if you just take out your
computer and show us a project or

00:56:36.921 --> 00:56:39.582
something that you've built,
